---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ActionLink from "../../../components/content/ActionLink.astro";
import ContentGrid from "../../../components/structure/ContentGrid.astro";
import ContentSection from "../../../components/structure/ContentSection.astro";
import PageBody from "../../../components/structure/PageBody.astro";
import PageHeading from "../../../components/structure/PageHeading.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../components/structure/SiteHeader.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Seattle Exhibits | Connections Museum"
  description="Follow a call through two floors of working switches, payphones, and teletypes at Connections Museum Seattle."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <PageBody>
    <PageHeading level="h1">Seattle exhibits</PageHeading>
    <p><ActionLink href="/seattle/visiting-info.html">Plan your visit to the Seattle museum</ActionLink></p>

    <ContentSection id="exhibits" title="A working museum" headingLevel="h2">
      <ContentGrid columns={2}>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            The museum sits inside a working telephone company central office in
            Georgetown, a building where calls are connected and routed.
            Volunteers keep switches that once connected whole
            neighborhoods ready to carry calls again. Hear the relays, watch the
            machinery move, and ask the person who works on it what just happened.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Herb Warrick put the idea for a telephone museum on paper in 1984,
            as the Bell System was breaking up and older switches were nearing
            retirement. Pacific Northwest Bell made room for the equipment and
            helped with the heavy frames. Don Ostrand and Telephone Pioneer
            volunteers began building the museum here in 1986, laying out,
            moving, and reconnecting the huge switch frames.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
      </ContentGrid>

      <p>
        You might find volunteers on ladders, pulling cable, soldering, testing,
        or chasing a fault. Keeping these switches working was a full-time job
        when they carried everyday calls, and it still takes skilled hands.
        Experienced telephone workers and newer volunteers pass that knowledge
        along as they keep the machines running. The big switches anchor the
        rooms, while smaller displays move and new functions come online. Take
        a tour or explore the public areas at your own pace; our volunteer
        docents are glad to talk through what you're seeing and what's changed
        since your last visit.
      </p>
    </ContentSection>

    <ContentGrid columns={2}>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="follow-a-call" title="Follow a call across both floors" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            Lift a receiver and dial. On the third floor, listen to selectors,
            motors, and relays find a path. Downstairs, see transistors and
            later integrated circuits fit more switching work into less room.
            Ask a volunteer docent to trace what each machine does with your call.
          </p>
          <ActionLink href="/seattle/exhibits/switches.html" prominence="feature">
            Explore the working switches
          </ActionLink>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="panel" title="The Panel switch" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            The 1923 Panel came from Seattle's old Rainier/Parkway office. Its
            sender remembers the number you dial; motor-driven selectors find
            a path. Volunteers moved and rewired this enormous machine, and
            it still connects calls here.
          </p>
          <ActionLink href="/seattle/exhibits/panel.html" prominence="feature">
            Read the Panel's story
          </ActionLink>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
    </ContentGrid>

    <ContentSection id="other-exhibit-categories" title="Payphones, teletypes, and more" headingLevel="h2">
      <p>
        There is more to a phone network than the switches. Public phones,
        teletypes, test gear, and the tools of line crews show how people used
        and cared for it. These smaller displays can change as volunteers add
        working functions or make room for something else.
      </p>
      <ActionLink href="/seattle/exhibits/other.html" prominence="feature">
        Explore phones, teletypes, and other exhibits
      </ActionLink>
    </ContentSection>
  </PageBody>
</BaseLayout>
